+
+    // Buffers whose staging-allocator read lock (see the acquire(DxvkAccess::Read) call sites in
+    // processVertices() and in processIndexBuffer()'s non-memoized path) was taken for THIS FRAME's
+    // draw calls only, so it can be released once EndFrame() has queued (on the CS thread, NOT
+    // synchronously -- see EndFrame()) a matching release ordered after this frame's
+    // commitGeometryToRT()/endFrame() CS commands. Without this, RtxStagingDataAlloc::alloc()'s
+    // isInUse() check never goes false and the allocator leaks a fresh MaxBufferSize block every time
+    // the current one fills.
+    //
+    // Deliberately NOT used for processIndexBuffer()'s MEMOIZED acquire (the D3D9CommonBuffer::
+    // RemixIboMemoizer cache hit path): that acquire is a long-lived hold for as long as the memoized
+    // slice may still be handed to a future frame's draw, not a per-frame one, so releasing it here
+    // would let a later frame's cache hit read staging memory this same allocator has already recycled
+    // for something else. See the comment at that acquire() call site for the full explanation.
+    //
+    // Guarded by a mutex: EndFrame() can be invoked from a caller that does not hold the D3D9 device
+    // lock (e.g. D3D9SwapChainEx::Present() calls it before acquiring the lock, and
+    // D3D9SwapChainEx::onWindowMessageEvent() calls it from whatever thread pumps window messages),
+    // so it is not safe to assume it is serialized against a concurrent draw call on another thread.
+    std::mutex m_stagingAcquiredMutex;
+    std::vector<Rc<DxvkBuffer>> m_stagingAcquired;
+
     D3D9DeviceEx* m_parent;
 
     std::optional<D3DPRESENT_PARAMETERS> m_activePresentParams;
